   "subject": "Jacobian adjustment term for logit-transformed estimation (ra_prospect, ra_noLA, ra_noRA)",
   "parts": [
    [
     1,
     "<div dir=\"ltr\">Hi all,<br><br>Thank you to the creators for putting together this great\n package! It&#39;s been invaluable while learning to implement STAN models. <br><br>I&#39;m\n currently focused on extracting subjective preference parameters via \nSTAN, as implemented in hBayesDM/ra_prospect. While researching the \ntopic, I found this article \n(<a href=\"http://mc-stan.org/users/documentation/case-studies/mle-params.html\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"nofollow\" data-saferedirecturl=\"https://www.google.com/url?hl=en-US&amp;q=http://mc-stan.org/users/documentation/case-studies/mle-params.html&amp;source=gmail&amp;ust=1793243280355000&amp;usg=AOvVaw14GlDg1p6UA0Y9pjt1SfjL\">http://mc-stan.org/users/documentation/case-studies/mle-params.html</a>) \ndescribing the importance/impact of including the Jacobian term when \ncalculating Bayesian estimates on logit transformed variables. I&#39;m still\n pretty new to STAN, but it appears the hBayesDM implementations of \nra_prospect, ra_noLA, and ra_noRA don&#39;t include this term.<br><br>I&#39;m \ntrying to wrap my head around the validity of including the Jacobian \nadjustment, and would greatly appreciate if anyone could provide insight\n to why it is not included in the hBayesDM implementation. <br><br>Thank you for your help,<br>Joe</div>"
    ]
   ]
  },
  {
   "id": "dFfbg7FOBAAJ",
   "from": "Haines, Nathaniel B.",
   "author": "47d1400f432367d7",
   "date": "2017-09-29T21:04:51Z",
   "subject": "Re: Jacobian adjustment term for logit-transformed estimation (ra_prospect, ra_noLA, ra_noRA)",
   "parts": [
    [
     1,
     "\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n<div>\r\n\r\n<div style=\"font-size:12pt;color:#000000;font-family:Arial,Helvetica,sans-serif\" dir=\"ltr\">\r\n<p>Hi Joe, </p>\r\n<p><br>\r\n</p>\r\n<p>This is a complicated issue, so I understand your confusion! however, if you look at the model in more detail described on the page you are linking:</p>\r\n<p><br>\r\n</p>\r\n<p></p>\r\n<pre style=\"box-sizing:border-box;overflow:auto;font-size:15px;padding:10.5px;margin-top:0px;margin-bottom:11px;line-height:1.42857;word-break:break-all;word-wrap:break-word;color:rgb(51,51,51);border:1px solid rgb(204,204,204);border-radius:4px\"><code style=\"box-sizing:border-box;font-size:inherit;padding:0px;color:inherit;background-color:transparent;border-radius:0px;white-space:pre-wrap\">data { \n  int&lt;lower=0&gt; N; \n  int&lt;lower=0, upper=1&gt; y[N]; \n} \nparameters { \n  real alpha; \n} \ntransformed parameters { \n  real&lt;lower=0, upper=1&gt; <span style=\"background-color:rgb(255,255,0)\">theta</span>; \n  <span style=\"background-color:rgb(255,255,0)\">theta</span> &lt;- inv_logit(alpha); \n} \nmodel { \n  for (n in 1:N) \n    y[n] ~ bernoulli(theta); \n  <span style=\"background-color:rgb(255,255,0)\">theta</span> ~ uniform(0, 1); \n} </code></pre>\r\n, you will see that theta is the transformed variable on the left side in the transformed parameters block, but also the same variable being distributed across in the model block. In this case, it is necessary to use the Jacobian adjustment. In hBayesDM, we\r\n do the following (from ra_noLA):\r\n<p></p>\r\n<p><br>\r\n</p>\r\n<p></p>\r\n<pre style=\"box-sizing:border-box;overflow:auto;font-size:15px;padding:10.5px;margin-top:0px;margin-bottom:11px;line-height:1.42857;word-break:break-all;word-wrap:break-word;color:rgb(51,51,51);border:1px solid rgb(204,204,204);border-radius:4px\"><code style=\"box-sizing:border-box;font-size:inherit;padding:0px;color:inherit;background-color:transparent;border-radius:0px;white-space:pre-wrap\"><div style=\"font-family:Arial,Helvetica,sans-serif,Helvetica,EmojiFont,&quot;Apple Color Emoji&quot;,&quot;Segoe UI Emoji&quot;,NotoColorEmoji,&quot;Segoe UI Symbol&quot;,&quot;Android Emoji&quot;,EmojiSymbols;font-size:16px\">transformed parameters {</div><div style=\"font-family:Arial,Helvetica,sans-serif,Helvetica,EmojiFont,&quot;Apple Color Emoji&quot;,&quot;Segoe UI Emoji&quot;,NotoColorEmoji,&quot;Segoe UI Symbol&quot;,&quot;Android Emoji&quot;,EmojiSymbols;font-size:16px\">  vector&lt;lower=0,upper=2&gt;[N] <span style=\"background-color:rgb(255,255,255)\">rho</span>;</div><div style=\"font-family:Arial,Helvetica,sans-serif,Helvetica,EmojiFont,&quot;Apple Color Emoji&quot;,&quot;Segoe UI Emoji&quot;,NotoColorEmoji,&quot;Segoe UI Symbol&quot;,&quot;Android Emoji&quot;,EmojiSymbols;font-size:16px\">  vector&lt;lower=0&gt;[N] <span style=\"background-color:rgb(255,255,255)\">tau</span>;</div><div style=\"font-family:Arial,Helvetica,sans-serif,Helvetica,EmojiFont,&quot;Apple Color Emoji&quot;,&quot;Segoe UI Emoji&quot;,NotoColorEmoji,&quot;Segoe UI Symbol&quot;,&quot;Android Emoji&quot;,EmojiSymbols;font-size:16px\">     </div><div style=\"font-family:Arial,Helvetica,sans-serif,Helvetica,EmojiFont,&quot;Apple Color Emoji&quot;,&quot;Segoe UI Emoji&quot;,NotoColorEmoji,&quot;Segoe UI Symbol&quot;,&quot;Android Emoji&quot;,EmojiSymbols;font-size:16px\">  for (i in 1:N) {</div><div style=\"font-family:Arial,Helvetica,sans-serif,Helvetica,EmojiFont,&quot;Apple Color Emoji&quot;,&quot;Segoe UI Emoji&quot;,NotoColorEmoji,&quot;Segoe UI Symbol&quot;,&quot;Android Emoji&quot;,EmojiSymbols;font-size:16px\">    <span style=\"background-color:rgb(255,255,255)\">rho</span>[i] = Phi_approx( mu_p[1] + sigma[1] * <span style=\"background-color:rgb(255,255,0)\">rho_p</span>[i] ) * 2;</div><div style=\"font-family:Arial,Helvetica,sans-serif,Helvetica,EmojiFont,&quot;Apple Color Emoji&quot;,&quot;Segoe UI Emoji&quot;,NotoColorEmoji,&quot;Segoe UI Symbol&quot;,&quot;Android Emoji&quot;,EmojiSymbols;font-size:16px\">  }</div><div style=\"font-family:Arial,Helvetica,sans-serif,Helvetica,EmojiFont,&quot;Apple Color Emoji&quot;,&quot;Segoe UI Emoji&quot;,NotoColorEmoji,&quot;Segoe UI Symbol&quot;,&quot;Android Emoji&quot;,EmojiSymbols;font-size:16px\">  <span style=\"background-color:rgb(255,255,255)\">tau</span><span style=\"background-color:rgb(255,255,255)\"> </span>= exp( mu_p[2] + sigma[2] * <span style=\"background-color:rgb(255,255,0)\">tau_p</span> );</div><div style=\"font-family:Arial,Helvetica,sans-serif,Helvetica,EmojiFont,&quot;Apple Color Emoji&quot;,&quot;Segoe UI Emoji&quot;,NotoColorEmoji,&quot;Segoe UI Symbol&quot;,&quot;Android Emoji&quot;,EmojiSymbols;font-size:16px\">}</div><div style=\"font-family:Arial,Helvetica,sans-serif,Helvetica,EmojiFont,&quot;Apple Color Emoji&quot;,&quot;Segoe UI Emoji&quot;,NotoColorEmoji,&quot;Segoe UI Symbol&quot;,&quot;Android Emoji&quot;,EmojiSymbols;font-size:16px\">model {</div><div style=\"font-family:Arial,Helvetica,sans-serif,Helvetica,EmojiFont,&quot;Apple Color Emoji&quot;,&quot;Segoe UI Emoji&quot;,NotoColorEmoji,&quot;Segoe UI Symbol&quot;,&quot;Android Emoji&quot;,EmojiSymbols;font-size:16px\">  # ra_prospect: Original model in Soko-Hessner et al 2009 PNAS</div><div style=\"font-family:Arial,Helvetica,sans-serif,Helvetica,EmojiFont,&quot;Apple Color Emoji&quot;,&quot;Segoe UI Emoji&quot;,NotoColorEmoji,&quot;Segoe UI Symbol&quot;,&quot;Android Emoji&quot;,EmojiSymbols;font-size:16px\">  # hyper parameters</div><div style=\"font-family:Arial,Helvetica,sans-serif,Helvetica,EmojiFont,&quot;Apple Color Emoji&quot;,&quot;Segoe UI Emoji&quot;,NotoColorEmoji,&quot;Segoe UI Symbol&quot;,&quot;Android Emoji&quot;,EmojiSymbols;font-size:16px\">  mu_p  ~ normal(0, 1.0); </div><div style=\"font-family:Arial,Helvetica,sans-serif,Helvetica,EmojiFont,&quot;Apple Color Emoji&quot;,&quot;Segoe UI Emoji&quot;,NotoColorEmoji,&quot;Segoe UI Symbol&quot;,&quot;Android Emoji&quot;,EmojiSymbols;font-size:16px\">  sigma ~ cauchy(0, 5.0);</div><div style=\"font-family:Arial,Helvetica,sans-serif,Helvetica,EmojiFont,&quot;Apple Color Emoji&quot;,&quot;Segoe UI Emoji&quot;,NotoColorEmoji,&quot;Segoe UI Symbol&quot;,&quot;Android Emoji&quot;,EmojiSymbols;font-size:16px\">  </div><div style=\"font-family:Arial,Helvetica,sans-serif,Helvetica,EmojiFont,&quot;Apple Color Emoji&quot;,&quot;Segoe UI Emoji&quot;,NotoColorEmoji,&quot;Segoe UI Symbol&quot;,&quot;Android Emoji&quot;,EmojiSymbols;font-size:16px\">  # individual parameters w/ Matt trick</div><div style=\"font-family:Arial,Helvetica,sans-serif,Helvetica,EmojiFont,&quot;Apple Color Emoji&quot;,&quot;Segoe UI Emoji&quot;,NotoColorEmoji,&quot;Segoe UI Symbol&quot;,&quot;Android Emoji&quot;,EmojiSymbols;font-size:16px\">  <span style=\"background-color:rgb(255,255,0)\">rho_p</span> ~ normal(0, 1.0);   </div><div style=\"font-family:Arial,Helvetica,sans-serif,Helvetica,EmojiFont,&quot;Apple Color Emoji&quot;,&quot;Segoe UI Emoji&quot;,NotoColorEmoji,&quot;Segoe UI Symbol&quot;,&quot;Android Emoji&quot;,EmojiSymbols;font-size:16px\">  <span style=\"background-color:rgb(255,255,0)\">tau_p</span> ~ normal(0, 1.0);</div><span style=\"font-family:Arial,Helvetica,sans-serif,Helvetica,EmojiFont,&quot;Apple Color Emoji&quot;,&quot;Segoe UI Emoji&quot;,NotoColorEmoji,&quot;Segoe UI Symbol&quot;,&quot;Android Emoji&quot;,EmojiSymbols;font-size:16px\"> ...</span> </code></pre>\r\n<div><br>\r\n</div>\r\nHere, you can see that we are distributing the normal along rho_p and tau_p, and then transforming these into rho and tau. In this case, the adjustment is not necessary. \r\n<div><br>\r\n</div>\r\n<div>I hope this helps! </div>\r\n<div><br>\r\n</div>\r\n<div>Best,</div>\r\n<div>Nate<br>\r\n<p></p>\r\n<p></p>\r\n<div><br>\r\n</div>\r\n<p></p>\r\n<p><br>\r\n</p>\r\n</div>\r\n</div>\r\n<hr style=\"display:inline-block;width:98%\">\r\n<div dir=\"ltr\"><font face=\"Calibri, sans-serif\" style=\"font-size:11pt\" color=\"#000000\"><b>From:</b> <a href data-email-masked rel=\"nofollow\">hbayesd...@googlegroups.com</a> &lt;<a href data-email-masked rel=\"nofollow\">hbayesd...@googlegroups.com</a>&gt; on behalf of Joseph Galaro &lt;<a href data-email-masked rel=\"nofollow\">jga...@gmail.com</a>&gt;<br>\r\n<b>Sent:</b> Friday, September 29, 2017 3:28:10 PM<br>\r\n<b>To:</b> hbayesdm-users<br>\r\n<b>Subject:</b> Jacobian adjustment term for logit-transformed estimation (ra_prospect, ra_noLA, ra_noRA)</font>\r\n<div> </div>\r\n</div>\r\n<div></div></div>"
    ],
    [
     3,
     "<div><div>\r\n<div dir=\"ltr\">Hi all,<br>\r\n<br>\r\nThank you to the creators for putting together this great package! It&#39;s been invaluable while learning to implement STAN models.\r\n<br>\r\n<br>\r\nI&#39;m currently focused on extracting subjective preference parameters via STAN, as implemented in hBayesDM/ra_prospect. While researching the topic, I found this article (<a href=\"http://mc-stan.org/users/documentation/case-studies/mle-params.html\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"nofollow\" data-saferedirecturl=\"https://www.google.com/url?hl=en-US&amp;q=http://mc-stan.org/users/documentation/case-studies/mle-params.html&amp;source=gmail&amp;ust=1793243280359000&amp;usg=AOvVaw3FYZNwkDZlVS3ddzN64HgL\">http://mc-stan.org/users/documentation/case-studies/mle-params.html</a>)\r\n describing the importance/impact of including the Jacobian term when calculating Bayesian estimates on logit transformed variables. I&#39;m still pretty new to STAN, but it appears the hBayesDM implementations of ra_prospect, ra_noLA, and ra_noRA don&#39;t include\r\n this term.<br>\r\n<br>\r\nI&#39;m trying to wrap my head around the validity of including the Jacobian adjustment, and would greatly appreciate if anyone could provide insight to why it is not included in the hBayesDM implementation.\r\n<br>\r\n<br>\r\nThank you for your help,<br>\r\nJoe</div>\r\n<p></p></div></div>"
    ],
    [
